The build root is site/, so the repository root can be the station's

> I do want to change the config to go to site for the canonical site. I think
> it's just the smart plan.

**⚠️ Change the Cloudflare dashboard field first, then merge. Not the other way
round, and not long apart.**

| Setting | was | now |
|---|---|---|
| Path / root directory | `/` | **`site`** |

Nothing else in the dashboard moves. The build command is still
`bundle exec jekyll build` and the output field is still `_site`, because that
field is relative to the root directory.

**Why the order matters, and why it is not the usual "a failed build is safe".**
The build command does not name a config. A build run at `/` after this merges
finds no `_config.yml`, takes the repository root as its source, and **publishes
something wrong while reporting success.** Measured earlier tonight: it prints
`Configuration file: none`, warns about a missing layout, and produces a broken
tree. A *failed* build is safe here — the last good deployment keeps serving — but
a successful wrong one is not.

## What moved, and why each one had to

`site/` is now self-contained, because each of these is read relative to the
directory the build runs in:

| | |
|---|---|
| `_config.yml` | Jekyll reads it from the working directory. Its `source: site` line is **gone** — the source is now simply where the config lives |
| `Gemfile` | bundler searches the working directory |
| `.ruby-version` | **the one that would have failed quietly.** Cloudflare's image reads it from the root directory setting, so left behind it would have moved the host onto their default Ruby — 3.4.4 — with nothing in CI noticing |
| `wrangler.jsonc` | wrangler looks from where it runs; `assets.directory: _site` now resolves to `site/_site` |

The repository root is left holding `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `advocate.yml`,
and the directories. That is the point rather than a side effect — **the root is
for the station, and the station is not the website.**

## Verified

- **The published bytes are identical.** Built before and after and compared file
  by file: all 67 files match, modulo the `?v=<mtime>` cache-busting token.
- All check suites pass, and two real breaks were found and fixed:
  - **`wrangler.jsonc` published on the first build.** It used to sit outside the
    source; now it is inside it. It is excluded, and — more to the point — **the
    publish guard did not catch it**, so it now knows about `wrangler.jsonc`,
    `Gemfile`, `Gemfile.lock` and `_config.yml` as well.
  - **`check-template.py` ran `bundle` from the repository root** to borrow the
    installed gems. There is no Gemfile there any more; it runs from the build
    root now, still pointing `--source` at the template.
- Workflows get `working-directory: site` on the Ruby setup and the build steps;
  `ruby-version:` points at `site/.ruby-version`; Azure's `app_location` is
  `site/_site`. `deploy-cloudflare.yml` needed nothing because #80 deleted it.
- Local development is now `cd site && bundle exec jekyll serve`, documented.

## Tidied on the way past, all pre-existing on `main`

- **Three doc links pointed at `CLAUDE.md`**, which was renamed to `AGENTS.md`
  without the links following. Fixed, along with the bare mentions.
- **One clause in your README** said the site is *"built from the repo root for
  the configs there"*, which this change makes untrue. Reworded to say `site/` is
  the build root — one clause, not a rewrite; the voice is yours.
- `docs/TENANCY.md`'s root inventory was stale twice over.

## Not in here

The journal engine. You said yes to it and that the first move is probably a
shared `/journal` on `main` while we stay with the `/site` and `/journal`
prefixes — and that the interesting version is the library treatment, where
journal content comes from a branch and **two blogs can run on one repository**,
which is how split-identity machines get their separateness. None of that is
attempted here. Mounting is still your pull request, and `docs/ADVOCATE.md` §6
still says the journal is not wanted, which is now the stale claim rather than
the live one.
